{
  "concept": "A system of a circle and a line can have zero, one, or two solutions, and which case occurs is determined entirely by the perpendicular distance from the circle's center to the line — equivalently by the sign of the discriminant of the resulting quadratic.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Three side-by-side panels sharing a common baseline",
    "Left panel: orange circle, fixed radius, centered in panel",
    "Center panel: green circle, same radius",
    "Right panel: red circle, same radius",
    "Blue double-headed arrow line in each panel, all with identical positive slope (~60° from horizontal), arrowheads at both ends",
    "Left panel line: passes above and to the left of the orange circle, no contact",
    "Center panel line: tangent to the green circle at its upper-left edge",
    "Right panel line: secant through the red circle, entering lower-left and exiting upper-right",
    "Filled black dot marking the tangency point in the center panel",
    "Filled black dots marking the two crossing points in the right panel (upper-right crossing emphasized)",
    "Caption labels beneath each panel: 'No solutions', 'One solution', 'Two solutions' in serif type",
    "Dashed perpendicular segment from circle center to the line (distance d) shown when active",
    "Radius segment r drawn from center in the active panel for comparison with d",
    "Equation panel (alternate view): circle equation x² + y² = r², line equation y = mx + b, substituted quadratic, discriminant expression and its sign",
    "Solution-count readout badge (0, 1, or 2) tied to the active configuration"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "lineOffset",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Line offset from center (distance d, in units of radius r)",
      "range": [0, 2, 0.02],
      "default": 1.6,
      "teaches": "Sliding the line parallel to itself makes intersection points appear, merge, and vanish — the count depends only on whether d is greater than, equal to, or less than the radius."
    },
    {
      "id": "caseStepper",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step through cases",
      "states": ["no solution", "one solution (tangent)", "two solutions (secant)"],
      "default": "no solution",
      "teaches": "The three panels are not three unrelated pictures but the three exhaustive outcomes of one circle-line system."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Geometric", "Algebraic"],
      "default": "Geometric",
      "teaches": "The visual number of crossings is the same information as the sign of the discriminant of the quadratic obtained by substitution."
    },
    {
      "id": "showDistance",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show distance d and radius r",
      "default": false,
      "teaches": "Comparing the perpendicular distance from center to line against the radius is the single test that decides the case."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Three possible outcomes",
      "narration": "Three circles, three parallel lines with the same slope. Only the line's distance from the center changes, and with it the number of shared points.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Geometric", "caseStepper": "no solution", "showDistance": false, "lineOffset": 1.6 }
    },
    {
      "title": "No solutions: the line misses",
      "narration": "The line passes entirely outside the orange circle. No point satisfies both equations, so the system has no solution.",
      "state": { "caseStepper": "no solution", "lineOffset": 1.6, "showDistance": true, "viewMode": "Geometric" }
    },
    {
      "title": "One solution: tangency",
      "narration": "Slide the line inward until d equals exactly the radius. The two would-be crossings merge into a single tangency point, marked in black.",
      "state": { "caseStepper": "one solution (tangent)", "lineOffset": 1.0, "showDistance": true, "viewMode": "Geometric" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two solutions: a secant",
      "narration": "Push the line closer still. It now cuts the circle, entering at the lower left and exiting at the upper right — two marked intersection points, two solutions.",
      "state": { "caseStepper": "two solutions (secant)", "lineOffset": 0.4, "showDistance": true, "viewMode": "Geometric" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The algebra behind the picture",
      "narration": "Substituting the line into the circle gives a quadratic. Its discriminant is negative, zero, or positive exactly when the line misses, touches, or cuts the circle.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Algebraic", "lineOffset": 0.4, "caseStepper": "two solutions (secant)", "showDistance": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep and watch the discriminant",
      "narration": "Drag the offset slider across the full range and watch the discriminant change sign at the tangent case — the geometric and algebraic tests are the same test.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Algebraic", "lineOffset": 1.0, "showDistance": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The number of solutions is decided by a single comparison: distance from center to line versus radius — d > r gives none, d = r gives one, d < r gives two, and this is precisely the sign of the discriminant.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Horizontal row of three equal-size panels on white. Circles are thin-stroked outlines in orange (left), green (center), red (right), all the same radius and vertically centered. Each panel carries one steel-blue double-headed arrow line at the same steep positive slope (~60°), drawn thin with arrowheads at both ends extending past the panel content. Left line clears the circle up and to the left; center line grazes the upper-left rim at one small filled black dot; right line slices through, with filled black dots at both crossings (upper-right one most prominent). Serif captions centered below each panel: 'No solutions', 'One solution', 'Two solutions'. When showDistance is on, draw a thin gray dashed perpendicular from the circle center to the line labeled d, plus a light gray radius segment labeled r, in the active panel. Active panel is emphasized (slightly enlarged, others dimmed) while stepping cases. In Algebraic view, replace the drawings with a clean centered equation card: x² + y² = r² above y = mx + b, then the substituted quadratic (1+m²)x² + 2mbx + (b² − r²) = 0, then D = 4m²b² − 4(1+m²)(b² − r²) with its numeric value color-coded red for D<0, black for D=0, green for D>0, and a large badge reading 0, 1, or 2 solutions."
}

